# 🛠️ Verilog to TL-Verilog Manual Refactoring Examples (Pen & Paper Conversion)

This repository contains manually converted TL-Verilog versions of selected Verilog modules. Each conversion was performed **manually on paper**, following the prompt flow methodology from [conversion-to-TLV](https://github.com/stevehoover/conversion-to-TLV). This exercise helped reinforce my understanding of how TL-Verilog models structure, timing, and control logic differently from traditional RTL.

## ✅ Converted Modules

### 🧱 Simple Modules

| Module        | Description                              | Notes |
|---------------|------------------------------------------|-------|
| 2:1 Mux (8-bit)| A basic data selector with 8-bit inputs  | Applied prompt steps for conditional logic & abstraction |
| 4-bit Counter | Synchronous counter with enable signal    | Explored pipelining and reset handling in TLV             |

### 🔧 Custom Design from Grad Project

| Module     | Description                        | Notes |
|------------|------------------------------------|-------|
| SPI Block  | SPI controller for serial I/O      | Manually mapped FSM + shift logic to pipeline-friendly TLV stages |

## 📖 Purpose

- Understand how prompt-based transformations evolve Verilog into TL-Verilog
- Practice modular decomposition and pipeline stage insertion
- Prepare for full integration with the `convert.py` toolchain
- Identify which prompt steps are ambiguous or complex in real-world scenarios

## 📂 Files

Each module folder contains:
- Original Verilog (.v) file
- Pen-and-paper translated TL-Verilog (.tlv or `.txt`)
- Notes or diagrams for key decisions taken during conversion

## 🧠 Based on:
- `prompts.json` from [conversion-to-TLV](https://github.com/stevehoover/conversion-to-TLV/blob/main/prompts.json)
- [Makerchip](https://makerchip.com) TL-Verilog learning modules
- M5 methodology (https://github.com/rweda/M5)

